## 2. Boring vs Sexy Assets

### The Core Thesis

> *"Return on invested capital is inversely correlated to how sexy an industry sounds. If your industry sounds exciting at a dinner party, your returns are probably going to be shit."*

**The Data:**
- **Waste Management ($WM):** 20-year CAGR ~12%, boring as dry toast
- **Procter & Gamble ($PG):** 20-year CAGR ~8%, sells soap
- **Tesla ($TSLA):** 8-year CAGR ~25% (2016–2024) but with 3 separate 50%+ drawdowns, extreme volatility
- **Carvana ($CVNA):** Went from $360 to $4 in 18 months — incredibly sexy concept, catastrophic returns for anyone who bought near peak
- **Peloton ($PTON):** $162 → $3. Sexy story. Terrible investment.
- **WeWork:** $47B valuation → bankruptcy. "Community-driven workspace." Sexy. Destroyed.

**The SAG-AFTRA Data Point:**

Galloway repeatedly cites SAG-AFTRA statistics to illustrate the glamour trap:

> *"SAG-AFTRA has 160,000 members. 90% of them are unemployed at any given time. The median annual income for a SAG-AFTRA member is under $10,000. Yet every year, thousands of people move to Los Angeles to pursue 'sexy.' The entertainment industry is the single worst risk-adjusted investment in America for 99% of people who enter it."*

**The Framework:**

| Industry | Perceived Sexiness | Actual ROIC (10yr avg) | Galloway Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Waste Management | 1/10 | 14.3% | Buy |
| Sanitary/Industrial Services | 1/10 | 12.8% | Buy |
| Insurance (Property/Casualty) | 2/10 | 11.5% | Buy |
| Consumer Staples | 3/10 | 10.2% | Buy |
| Energy (Midstream) | 3/10 | 9.8% | Buy |
| REITs (Industrial) | 4/10 | 9.5% | Buy |
| Enterprise Software | 6/10 | 8.1% | Selective |
| Cloud Infrastructure | 7/10 | 6.2% | Cautious |
| Electric Vehicles | 8/10 | -2.3% | Avoid |
| Crypto/Blockchain | 9/10 | N/A (speculative) | Avoid |
| Entertainment/Media | 8/10 | 3.1% | Avoid |
| SPACs (2020–21) | 10/10 | -40% avg return | Avoid |

**The "Glamour Tax":**
Galloway argues that sexy industries attract so much capital (both human and financial) that returns get competed down to zero or negative:

> *"When everyone is trying to get into the same room, the room gets crowded, the air gets thin, and the smartest people in the room still lose money because there are too many of them. The best investment you can make is in an industry that makes people at cocktail parties yawn."*

### 90% Unemployment Data in "Glamour" Fields

Beyond SAG-AFTRA, Galloway applies this logic broadly:

1. **Professional Sports:** ~2% of NCAA athletes go pro. Of those, 50% are bankrupt within 5 years of retirement.
2. **Restaurant Entrepreneurship:** 60% fail within 3 years. 80% within 10 years.
3. **Startup Founders (non-VC-backed):** 75% of small businesses fail within 10 years.
4. **Film Production:** 90% of independent films never break even.
5. **Music Industry:** 99% of artists on Spotify earn less than $50,000/year.

> *"The correlation is brutal: the more people want to do something, the worse the financial outcomes for the average participant. Follow the boring. Follow the capital-intensive, ugly, regulated industries that smart people are trying to escape, not enter."*

