## 3. Personal Asset Allocation

### The Galloway Portfolio

| Asset Class | Target Allocation | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Real Estate | 40% | Tangible, tax-advantaged, inflation hedge |
| Private Equity/Venture | 40% | Illiquidity premium, control, asymmetric upside |
| Public Equities | 15–20% | Liquidity, diversification, covered call income |
| Cash/Equivalents | 0–5% | Optionality, dry powder for dislocations |

> *"My personal portfolio is roughly 40% real estate, 40% private, and 15–20% public. That's not advice — that's just what I do. Most people should be more in public equities. But I have an edge in private markets, so I lean into it."*

### The 3–5% Per Asset Rule

**The Rule:** No single asset (outside your primary residence) should represent more than 3–5% of your total net worth.

> *"The 3–5% rule is the single most important risk management tool I have. If I'm wrong about any single investment — and I'm wrong a lot — it doesn't matter because no single bet can hurt me. The exception is your primary home, which can be 15–20% because it's not just an investment: it's where you live."*

**Rationale:**
- At 3–5%, a complete loss of any single position reduces net worth by ~3–5%
- At 3–5%, a 10-bagger from one position moves your net worth by ~30–50%
- Asymmetric upside, capped downside
- Forces disciplined exit: when a position grows to 10%+ of portfolio due to appreciation, you must trim back to 5%

### Primary Home Exception (15–20%)

> *"Your primary residence is different. It's a consumption asset that happens to appreciate. Don't kid yourself — you're not going to sell it at the top and rent. You need to live somewhere. So treat it as a 15–20% allocation maximum, but understand that 3–5% rule doesn't apply because the utility is different."*

**Galloway's Home-Buying Rules:**
1. Buy in a city with strong job growth (in-migration > 3%/year)
2. Buy in a supply-constrained market (coastal, zoning-restricted)
3. 30-year fixed mortgage at sub-5% rates (acknowledged as near-impossible post-2023)
4. Never buy more house than 3× household income
5. Live in it for at least 10 years

### Rebalancing with Goldman Sachs

**How Galloway Does It:**

> *"I have a relationship with Goldman Sachs. Their private wealth management team handles rebalancing for my public portfolio. They charge me a fee — it's not zero — but what I'm paying for is discipline. When I want to buy more of something because it's gone up 40% and I feel like a genius, they force me to sell it back to 5%. When something has dropped 60% and I feel like an idiot, they force me to buy more. The fee is worth it because it replaces my lack of stoicism with institutional process."*

**Rebalancing Mechanics:**
- **Frequency:** Quarterly
- **Trigger:** Any position exceeding 7% of the public portfolio triggers automatic trimming back to 5%
- **Tax Management:** Tax-loss harvesting within the framework
- **Cash Rebalancing:** When cash exceeds 5%, deploy into the asset class that has underperformed most recently
- **Lifecycle Adjustments:** As net worth grows, private allocation increases (illiquidity becomes more tolerable)

> *"Rich people don't need as much liquidity. If your net worth is $50M and you have $40M in real estate and private equity, you still have $10M in public markets and cash. That's more liquidity than most people will ever have. Liquidity is a function of absolute dollars, not percentages."*

