## 5. Case Studies

### 5.1 Flight Lease Capital — 30% IRR

**The Investment:**
- **Asset:** Aircraft leasing company
- **Thesis:** Capital-intensive, boring, high-barrier-to-entry industry operated by "gearheads in Florida"
- **Entry:** ~$5M investment
- **Outcome:** Multiple exits generating ~30% IRR over 7 years

> *"Flight Lease Capital was run by a guy in Florida who just knew more about aircraft engines than anyone else. He wasn't sexy. He wasn't on podcasts. He just understood that a GE90 engine on a 777 has a certain residual value curve and he could model it better than the banks. That's it. That's the edge. And it generated 30% IRR because nobody wanted to do something as boring as aircraft leasing."*

**Why It Worked:**
1. **Boring industry:** Aircraft leasing is capital-intensive, regulated, and requires domain expertise
2. **Gearhead advantage:** Deep technical knowledge of aircraft maintenance cycles, residual values, lease terms
3. **Counter-cyclical entry:** Entered after a downturn when aircraft values were depressed
4. **Capital structure:** Debt was secured against physical assets (planes) that don't disappear
5. **Exit multiple expansion:** As the portfolio grew, larger institutional buyers paid higher multiples for scale

**Key Numbers:**
- 30% IRR over 7 years
- ~3× multiple on invested capital
- Portfolio of ~50 aircraft at peak
- Debt-to-equity ratio: ~3:1 (conservative for aircraft leasing)

### 5.2 Enjoy — $2.5M → $75M (FDA Moat)

**The Investment:**
- **Company:** Enjoy (formerly e-cigarette/vaping company)
- **Entry:** $2.5M
- **Exit:** ~$75M
- **Multiple:** ~30×
- **Timeframe:** ~5 years

> *"Enjoy was a vaping company. We invested $2.5M and got $75M back. Why? Because they had an FDA regulatory moat. The FDA made it nearly impossible for new competitors to enter the market. Existing players with approved applications had a protected oligopoly. It was ugly. It was regulated. It was perfect."*

**The FDA Moat:**
The FDA's Pre-Market Tobacco Application (PMTA) process created an almost insurmountable barrier to entry:
- Cost per application: $1M–$5M
- Time to approval: 2–4 years
- Scientific data requirements: multi-year clinical studies
- Result: After the PMTA deadline, effectively no new competitors could enter

**The Altria Buyout:**
- Altria (Philip Morris parent) acquired a significant stake
- The regulatory moat was the primary acquisition rationale
- Galloway exited via the buyout at ~30× his entry

> *"Regulation is the best moat. It's better than technology, better than brand, better than network effects — because regulation is enforced by the US government. You can't compete against that."*

### 5.3 Neva — $3M, Failed, Snowflake Acqui-hire

**The Investment:**
- **Company:** Neva (data infrastructure startup)
- **Entry:** ~$3M
- **Outcome:** Failed as a standalone business → acqui-hired by Snowflake
- **Return:** ~1× (original capital returned, minimal profit)

> *"Neva failed. It didn't work. The product wasn't right, the timing was off, and the team couldn't find product-market fit. But Snowflake bought the team for ~$3M, which meant we got our money back. A failure that returns your capital is a win in venture capital."*

**Lessons from Neva:**
1. **Failure is part of the asset class:** Galloway's private portfolio assumes 30–40% of investments will fail
2. **Talent retains value:** Even when a product fails, a good team can be acqui-hired
3. **Capital preservation first:** Structure deals to have downside protection where possible
4. **Snowflake's acqui-hire strategy:** Snowflake needed data engineering talent — the Neva team was worth $3M even without the product

> *"Most people only talk about their wins. I talk about Neva because it's more representative of how venture actually works. Most of my investments return 0–1×. A few return 3–5×. One in twenty returns 30×. You need the portfolio construction to survive the zeros to get to the thirties."*

### 5.4 Section — EdTech, COVID Boom/Crash, AI Pivot

**The Investment:**
- **Company:** Section (executive education platform)
- **Thesis:** Democratize executive education (MBA-level content at lower cost)
- **COVID Effect:** Massive boom — remote learning demand exploded
- **Post-COVID:** Sharp contraction — in-person learning returned, enterprise budgets tightened
- **AI Pivot:** Repositioned as AI-powered executive education

> *"Section was an edtech play. During COVID, everyone wanted online executive education. Revenue went vertical. After COVID, everyone wanted to go back to Harvard Business School in person. Revenue went horizontal, then down. The team pivoted to AI before it was trendy, and now they're in a stronger position. But the ride was brutal."*

**The Numbers:**
- Pre-COVID valuation: ~$15M
- COVID peak valuation: ~$50M+
- Post-COVID trough: ~$15–20M
- Current (post-AI pivot): Recovering

**Key Takeaways:**
1. **Macro timing matters:** EdTech was a COVID beneficiary and a post-COVID victim
2. **Founder resilience:** The team survived the downturn and pivoted
3. **AI as second act:** AI-powered executive education is a credible re-thesis
4. **Patience in private markets:** Section is still a hold — the story isn't over

