## 6. Real Estate Strategy

### The 0.1% Strategy

> *"Real estate is not about leverage. It's about location. And the only locations that matter are the 0.1% of global real estate that the world's wealthy want to live in."*

**The 0.1% Locations:**
1. **London** — Mayfair, Knightsbridge, Belgravia, Chelsea
2. **New York City** — Manhattan (Upper East Side, Tribeca, SoHo)
3. **Palm Beach** — "Billionaire's Bunker" (post-COVID migration hub)
4. **Aspen** — Ultra-luxury mountain retreat

> *"There are 8 billion people on Earth. The 8 million richest ones — 0.1% — all want to own a piece of the same 4 zip codes. London, New York, Palm Beach, Aspen. That demand isn't going away. It's only increasing. There is no more land being made in these places. Supply is fixed. Demand grows with global GDP. The math is inescapable."*

**Why These 4 Cities:**
- **Supply-constrained:** Zoning, historical preservation, and geography (islands, mountains, coastlines) prevent new construction
- **Global demand:** Buyers from Russia, China, Middle East, Europe, and Latin America all want a foothold
- **Currency hedge:** International buyers treat these properties as a store of value outside their home currency
- **Appreciation floor:** Even in a down market, these properties decline less and recover faster than any other real estate

### Tax Advantages

**1. Depreciation (2–3% per year)**

> *"The IRS lets you depreciate a building over 27.5 years — roughly 3.6% per year. Except in the 0.1% locations, buildings don't depreciate. They appreciate. So you get a tax deduction for something that isn't happening. It's a legal, government-sanctioned arbitrage. I'll take it."*

- Residential real estate: 27.5-year depreciation schedule (~3.6%/year)
- Commercial real estate: 39-year depreciation schedule (~2.6%/year)
- Bonus depreciation: Cost segregation studies can accelerate depreciation in early years
- Impact: On a $5M property, annual depreciation deduction of ~$140K–$180K

**2. 1031 Exchanges (Like-Kind Exchanges)**

> *"A 1031 exchange lets you sell a property, take all the proceeds, buy another property, and pay zero tax on the gain. You can do this forever. You die, and your heirs get a step-up in basis. Zero capital gains tax. Ever. This is the single greatest wealth-building mechanism in the US tax code."*

- **Galloway's 1031 track record:** Has executed at least 3 1031 exchanges, deferring millions in capital gains
- **Rules:** Must identify replacement property within 45 days, close within 180 days
- **Qualifying properties:** Any held for business or investment use (not primary residence)
- **Step-up at death:** Heirs receive the property at its fair market value on date of death, wiping out all deferred gains

**3. No Mortgages**

> *"I pay cash for real estate. No mortgages. Zero debt. I want the cash flow, the appreciation, and the tax benefits without the risk of a bank calling my loan. When the 2008 crisis hit, people with leverage lost everything. People with cash bought everything. I want to be the buyer, not the seller, during the next crisis."*

**Galloway's No-Debt Rule:**
- All real estate purchased with cash
- No mortgage interest deduction needed because depreciation already covers taxable income
- Zero risk of margin call, foreclosure, or lender covenant violation
- Trade-off: Lower leverage means lower nominal returns but much higher risk-adjusted returns

> *"Leverage amplifies returns on the way up. It also amplifies losses on the way down. I've been through enough cycles to know that the people who get wiped out are never the ones who own the assets. It's the ones who borrowed to own the assets. I sleep better with no debt."*

### Additional Real Estate Rules

1. **Buy and hold forever:** "There is no 'sell' button on my real estate. Only 'buy' and 'collect rent.'"
2. **Location over property:** "I'd rather own the worst building on the best block than the best building on the worst block."
3. **Cash flow over appreciation:** "Appreciation is gravy. Cash flow is the meal."
4. **10-year minimum hold:** "If you can't hold it for 10 years, don't buy it."

