## 7. The Red Envelope Disaster

### The All-In Fallacy

> *"The worst investment mistake I ever made was going all-in. The 'all-in' mentality — placing a huge bet on a single narrative because you are certain you're right — is the single most destructive force in investing. It's also glorified by Silicon Valley."*

**The Company:** Red Envelope (early 2000s online gift retailer)
**The Mistake:** Galloway was so confident in the thesis that he concentrated an outsized percentage of his net worth in the stock.

### The Sequence of Events

**1. The Warehouse Address Error**
> *"I was on the board. The company had 8 warehouses around the country to ensure two-day delivery before Amazon made two-day delivery standard. Except the labels on the products going to the warehouses had the wrong address. Not wrong warehouse — wrong city. Product was being shipped everywhere except where it needed to be. I was on the board and I didn't catch it. That's on me."*

**2. The Longshoreman Strike**
> *"Then the West Coast longshoremen went on strike. Ports shut down. The Christmas inventory — which was the entire year's profit — was sitting on ships in the Pacific. You can't deliver Christmas presents from a shipping container floating in international waters."*

**3. The Credit Line Cut**
> *"The bank called the credit line. Not reduced. Not renegotiated. Cut. Zero. On December 15th. Two weeks before Christmas. The company had no cash, no inventory, and no way to borrow."*

**4. The Stock Collapse**
> *"The stock went from $7 to Chapter 11. In two weeks. I lost millions. Not just the investment — I lost conviction in my own judgment. It took me years to recover financially and psychologically."*

| Event | Stock Price | Timeframe |
|---|---|---|
| Peak confidence | ~$7 | Pre-disaster |
| Warehouse error revealed | ~$5 | Week 1 |
| Longshoreman strike announced | ~$3 | Week 1 |
| Credit line cut | ~$1 | Week 2 |
| Chapter 11 filing | $0 | Week 2 |

### The Lesson

> *"Red Envelope taught me everything I know about risk management. Not from theory — from the experience of watching $7 become $0 in two weeks. There is no such thing as a sure thing. There is no such thing as 'all-in.' The market doesn't care about your convictions. It cares about your positioning."*

**Galloway's 3 Rules Born from Red Envelope:**
1. **Never let any single position exceed 5% of net worth.** "If Red Envelope had been 5% of my portfolio, I'd have lost 5%. It wasn't. I lost way more."
2. **Assume every thesis is wrong.** "If Red Envelope taught me anything, it's that a thesis is just a story you tell yourself until reality shows up with different facts."
3. **The market can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent.** "I was right about the thesis — online gifting was going to be huge. I was right about the timing. I was wrong about the execution risk, and execution risk can kill you no matter how good your thesis is."

