## 8. Covered Calls Strategy

### The Core Strategy

> *"I sell weekly out-of-the-money covered calls on every single public equity position I own. Every week. Without fail. It's not exciting. It generates 3–5% annualized income on top of dividends and appreciation. But it's not the income that matters — it's the discipline."*

**The Mechanics:**
1. Own 100+ shares of a stock
2. Sell a call option with a strike price above the current market price
3. Expiration: 1 week out (7 days)
4. Strike: ~5–10% above current price (out-of-the-money)
5. Collect premium immediately
6. If stock stays below strike: keep premium, repeat next week
7. If stock exceeds strike: shares get called away, you sell at your target price + keep premium

> *"I'm collecting dimes in front of a bulldozer. The bulldozer is the stock going up 20% in a week and my shares getting called away. But I don't care about the 20% weeks. I care about the 52 weeks of consistent income and the forced discipline of having to look at my portfolio every single week."*

### The $15M Loss from Naked Calls

> *"I deviated from the strategy once. I sold naked calls — calls without owning the underlying stock. I thought I was being smart, 'optimizing' the strategy. Instead of collecting dimes, I was betting against the market with no protection. The market turned against me. $15M gone. Because I got greedy."*

**The Deviation:**
- **Strategy:** Naked call selling (unlimited downside risk)
- **Reason:** Greed — naked calls generate more premium than covered calls
- **Result:** Market reversed, stock skyrocketed, Galloway was forced to cover at catastrophic prices
- **Loss:** ~$15M
- **Timeframe:** A few weeks

> *"I violated my own rules. I went from 'collecting dimes in front of a bulldozer' to 'standing in front of a bulldozer holding dimes.' The strategy wasn't broken. I was. The $15M tuition payment taught me to never deviate from the system."*

### Why Weekly OTM Covered Calls?

**Advantages of Weekly (vs Monthly):**
1. **Higher annualized premium:** Weekly options have higher theta decay (time decay accelerates as expiration approaches)
2. **More frequent discipline:** Forces weekly portfolio review
3. **Lower gap risk:** Only exposed to 7 days of price movement instead of 30
4. **More granular strike selection:** Can adjust strike prices weekly based on market conditions

**Disadvantages Acknowledged:**
1. **Higher transaction costs:** 52 trades per year vs 12
2. **More time-intensive:** Requires weekly attention
3. **Capped upside:** If stock jumps 20% in a week, capped at strike + premium

> *"I'm okay with capped upside. I've never gotten rich from a single week of 20% gains. I've gotten rich from 30 years of 9% annual returns. The covered calls add 3–5% to that. Compounded over 30 years, that's the difference between a good retirement and a great one."*

