## 12. Worst Prediction

### Panic-Selling After Trump's 2016 Election

> *"My worst prediction was not about a stock. It was about the entire market. On November 8, 2016, Donald Trump was elected President. I was convinced — absolutely, viscerally certain — that the market would crash. I panic-sold almost all my stocks. I destroyed 25% of my wealth."*

**The Timeline:**
- **November 8, 2016:** Trump wins. Galloway sells.
- **November 9, 2016:** S&P 500 futures hit limit down overnight. Galloway feels vindicated.
- **November 9–10, 2016:** Markets reverse. The S&P 500 closes up on November 9.
- **2017:** The S&P 500 returns 21.8%.
- **2018:** Another strong year (despite Q4 volatility).
- **2016–2020 cumulative S&P 500 return:** ~85%.

> *"I was right for exactly one night. I felt like a genius at 2 AM on November 9th. By the close of business on November 9th, I was an idiot. I had sold into a panic that lasted 12 hours. The cost of that 12-hour panic was 25% of my net worth that I never got back."*

### The Deeper Lesson

> *"The worst investment decision I ever made wasn't about a stock pick. It was about letting my political beliefs override my investment principles. I hated Trump. I thought his election would destroy the economy. I was wrong about both. And my wrongness cost me millions. The lesson is: your portfolio doesn't care about your politics. The market doesn't care about your feelings. Only about earnings, interest rates, and time."*

**The Stoicism Failure:**
- Galloway violated the Stoicism variable in his own equation
- He let emotion (fear, political anger) override process
- He made a binary bet on a single outcome (crash) instead of staying diversified
- He proved his own thesis: without stoicism, time and diversification don't work because you sell at exactly the wrong moment

> *"I was the guy I warn everyone not to be. I was the guy who said 'this time is different.' It wasn't different. It never is. And I paid for my hubris with 25% of my wealth. I'm embarrassed to tell this story. But I tell it because if someone with my background and experience can make this mistake, anyone can. And the only defense is process, process, process."*

