## 14. 20-Criteria Investment Rubric

### Overview

Galloway uses a 5-level rubric across 20 criteria to evaluate any potential investment. The rubric is scored 1–5 (5 = best) across four domains:

1. **Data & Thesis** (5 criteria)
2. **Valuation & Entry** (5 criteria)
3. **Execution & Risk Management** (5 criteria)
4. **Portfolio Fit & Psychology** (5 criteria)

**Total Possible Score:** 100 (20 × 5)

> *"A score below 60 means don't invest. A score of 60–75 means maybe, but watch carefully. A score of 75–90 means invest — your thesis is solid. A score above 90 means you're overconfident and probably missing something. Go back and check your assumptions."*

### Domain 1: Data & Thesis (5 Criteria)

| # | Criterion | 1 (Avoid) | 3 (Adequate) | 5 (Strong) | Weight |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Market Size | < $500M TAM | $1B–$10B TAM | $50B+ TAM | 1× |
| 2 | Competitive Moat | None (commodity) | Moderate (brand, scale) | Regulatory/Network/Patent | 2× |
| 3 | Revenue Visibility | < 6 months | 12–24 months | 3+ years (contracts, subs) | 1× |
| 4 | Management Quality | Founders have exited, weak board | Good operator, some gaps | Domain expert + proven operator | 2× |
| 5 | Industry Sexiness | "Sexy" (all competitors are in) | Neutral | "Boring" (capital-intensive, regulated) | 1× |

### Domain 2: Valuation & Entry (5 Criteria)

| # | Criterion | 1 (Avoid) | 3 (Adequate) | 5 (Strong) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 6 | Revenue Multiple | > 20× public comps | 10–20× public comps | < 10× public comps |
| 7 | Growth-Adjusted Valuation | PEG > 3 | PEG 1–2 | PEG < 1 |
| 8 | Entry Discount | At market, no premium | 10–20% below last round | 20%+ below (distress/counter-cyclical) |
| 9 | Downside Protection | None (common equity, no preferences) | Pro-rata rights, information rights | Liquidation preference, board seat |
| 10 | Path to Exit | Unknown / > 10 years | IPO/acquisition window in 5–7 years | Clear acquirer or IPO path in 3–5 years |

### Domain 3: Execution & Risk Management (5 Criteria)

| # | Criterion | 1 (Avoid) | 3 (Adequate) | 5 (Strong) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 11 | Product-Market Fit | Pre-revenue, no customers | Early revenue, some PMF signals | Strong PMF, >$10M ARR, 100%+ NRR |
| 12 | Unit Economics | Negative gross margin | Gross margin 40–60% | Gross margin > 60%, LTV/CAC > 5× |
| 13 | Capital Efficiency | Burning cash with no path to breakeven | Path to breakeven in 12–18 months | Profitable or near-profitable |
| 14 | Regulatory Risk | High (potential existential threat) | Moderate (compliance cost) | Low (regulated industry = moat) |
| 15 | Key-Man Risk | Entire business depends on 1 person | Some dependency but team in place | Institutionalized, no single point of failure |

### Domain 4: Portfolio Fit & Psychology (5 Criteria)

| # | Criterion | 1 (Avoid) | 3 (Adequate) | 5 (Strong) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 16 | Position Sizing | Would need > 5% of NW | 3–5% of NW | < 3% of NW |
| 17 | Correlation to Existing Holdings | High (same sector/thesis as current) | Moderate | Low (adds diversification) |
| 18 | Holding Period Match | < 3 years (forced exit likely) | 5–7 years | 10+ years (matches time horizon) |
| 19 | Psychological Comfort | "Keeps me up at night" | "Slight unease" | "Could lose entire investment and not care" |
| 20 | Stoicism Stress Test | Would panic-sell at -30% | Would hold at -30%, worry at -50% | Would buy more at -50% |

### Scoring Guide

> *"The rubric is not a science. It's a framework for making sure you haven't missed something obvious. If you score a 4 on 'Management Quality' but a 1 on 'Regulatory Risk,' the rubric forces you to weigh the two against each other. The weighted scoring helps, but the real value is the conversation the rubric creates — with your co-investors, with your partners, with yourself."*

**Galloway's Weighted Scoring System:**
- Criteria 2 (Moat) and 4 (Management) are 2× weighted
- All others are 1× weighted
- Maximum weighted score: 100

**Interpretation:**

| Weighted Score | Action |
|---|---|
| 80–95 | Invest — thesis is strong across all dimensions |
| 65–79 | Invest cautiously — size position smaller than normal |
| 50–64 | Pass — too many risks, not enough edge |
| < 50 | Hard pass — thesis is broken |

