## 15. The 27 Questions Framework

Galloway reportedly asks 27 questions before making an investment. These questions encompass the entirety of his analytical framework. Below is the complete list, organized by domain.

### Market & Industry (Q1–Q5)

1. **What is the total addressable market (TAM) and is it growing or shrinking?**
   > *"A great company in a shrinking market is a bad investment. A mediocre company in a growing market is a great investment."*

2. **Who are the top 3 competitors and what are their market shares?**
   > *"If you can't name 3 competitors without Googling, you don't understand the market."*

3. **What is the regulatory environment — is it a moat or a threat?**
   > *"Regulation can be your best friend (Enjoy) or your worst enemy (Uber in the early days). Know which one you're buying."*

4. **Is this a 'sexy' industry?**
   > *"If every VC in Silicon Valley is trying to invest here, find a different industry."*

5. **What is the gross margin structure of the industry — not just the company?**
   > *"Industry structure determines the ceiling. Company execution determines how close you get to the ceiling."*

### Business Model & Economics (Q6–Q10)

6. **What is the unit of revenue?**
   > *"Do you sell a product once, or do you sell a subscription? Recurring revenue is worth 3–5× more per dollar than one-time revenue."*

7. **What is customer acquisition cost (CAC) and how is it trending?**
   > *"CAC going up is a leading indicator of trouble. It means competition is getting more intense or your marketing is getting less efficient."*

8. **What is customer lifetime value (LTV) and LTV/CAC ratio?**
   > *"LTV/CAC below 3× means you're printing money for Google and Facebook, not for yourself."*

9. **What is net revenue retention (NRR)?**
   > *"NRR above 120% means your customers are expanding faster than they're leaving. NRR below 80% means your product is decaying and you don't know it yet."*

10. **What is the margin structure and how does it scale?**
    > *"Software companies at 80% gross margins can grow into profitability. Services companies at 30% gross margins are a treadmill."*

### Competitive Advantage (Q11–Q15)

11. **What is the moat — technology, brand, regulation, network effects, or scale?**
    > *"If you can't articulate the moat in one sentence, it doesn't exist."*

12. **Is the moat getting stronger or weaker over time?**
    > *"Most moats erode. Ask yourself: is this company more defensible today than it was a year ago?"*

13. **What would have to be true for a competitor to kill this business?**
    > *"Think like an attacker. If you were a well-funded new entrant, how would you destroy this company? If the answer is 'with $10M and a good team,' the moat is weak."*

14. **What does the switching cost look like for customers?**
    > *"High switching costs are the best moat. If a customer can leave in 30 minutes with no cost, you don't have a business — you have a rental."*

15. **Is there a network effect — does the product get better with more users?**
    > *"Network effects are the only durable moat in tech because they get stronger over time. Everything else eventually gets competed away."*

### Management & Team (Q16–Q19)

16. **Has the CEO done this before — specifically this industry, this stage?**
    > *"First-time CEOs are not a red flag. First-time CEOs who don't know their industry are."*

17. **How much of the CEO's net worth is in this company?**
    > *"If the CEO isn't all-in financially, why should you be?"*

18. **Who is on the board and what is their relevant experience?**
    > *"A board of celebrities is a liability. A board of people who have actually built businesses in this industry is an asset."*

19. **What happens if the CEO leaves tomorrow?**
    > *"If the answer is 'the company falls apart,' you have key-man risk. Price it accordingly or don't invest."*

### Valuation & Entry (Q20–Q22)

20. **What is the current valuation and what does it imply about future growth?**
    > *"A 20× revenue multiple implies the market expects you to be the next Amazon. Most companies are not the next Amazon."*

21. **What is the historical valuation trajectory — is it going up or down?**
    > *"Down rounds destroy morale, kill option pools, and alienate early investors. A company raising at a flat valuation is not good news."*

22. **What is the entry discount relative to other investors?**
    > *"If you're paying the same price as everyone else, you have no edge. Your edge is your strategic value — use it to negotiate a discount."*

### Risk & Scenarios (Q23–Q25)

23. **What happens to this business in a recession?**
    > *"COVID was a stress test. How did the company perform? If revenue went up during COVID, ask if it's sustainable. If revenue went down, ask why."*

24. **What is the regulatory risk — from antitrust, data privacy, trade policy, or industry-specific regulation?**
    > *"The worst-case regulatory scenario is existential (e.g., TikTok ban, Juul ban). Ask yourself: how much of the current valuation would survive a regulatory worst case?"*

25. **What is the technology risk — will this be obsolete in 5 years?**
    > *"Technology has a half-life. Question everything. Especially AI-native companies that could be disrupted by the next model release."*

### Personal Fit (Q26–Q27)

26. **Would I be comfortable owning this at a 50% drawdown without selling?**
    > *"If the answer is no, your position size is too large. Size down until the answer is yes."*

27. **Am I investing because I believe in the thesis or because I'm afraid of missing out (FOMO)?**
    > *"FOMO is the most expensive emotion in finance. If you're investing because you can't stand to watch someone else get rich, stop. FOMO-driven investments lose money 80% of the time."*

